tely below, 3.8 contracts would be expected. The observed-to-expected ratio is 15.8, with a 95% confidence interval of 9.3 to 28.0. December accounts for 0% of the contracts just under the limit, compared with 2% of all its services and supplies contracts. The awardee with the most contracts just under the limit, called awardee A here, has 5. Contract [1], for 14,990 euros, was awarded in November 2025.\nWhat it cannot show: The concentration of contracts in December does not indicate improper activity. Similar amounts under the limit can result from market pricing or budget constraints. The high ratio may reflect normal variation rather than wrongdoing.\nWhat can be checked: Check the contracts' dates and awardees to see if multiple contracts serve the same purpose. Review the awardee A's contracts to determine if they relate to the same need. Contract [1] shows a single low-value contract, which may be part of a pattern.","origen_es":"modelo","origen_en":"modelo","modelo":"Qwen3-4B (Q4_K_M, llama.cpp)","regla":"ss","citas":["RCD%2FDVU1K5DjHF5qKI4aaw%3D%3D","k32OXG9RXWyKeVWTb9Scog%3D%3D","MdqvTcWp4Pa5HQrHoP3G5A%3D%3D"],"generado":"2026-11-15"}}